<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04G3">Variant <span data-tag="04G3">1.3.2.8</span>.</a> A <em>nonunital monoid</em> is a set $M$ equipped with a map </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  m: M \times M \rightarrow M \quad \quad (x,y) \mapsto xy \]
</div>
<p> which satisfies the associative law $x(yz) = (xy)z$ for $x,y,z \in M$. If $M$ and $M'$ are nonunital monoids, a function $f: M \rightarrow M'$ is a <em>nonunital monoid homomorphism</em> if it satisfies the equation $f(xy) = f(x) f(y)$ for every pair of elements $x,y \in M$. We let $\operatorname{Mon}^{\operatorname{nu}}$ denote the category whose objects are nonunital monoids and whose morphisms are nonunital monoid homomorphisms. </p>
</article>
